text:
I was placed in a poor cradle,

badly swaddled in coarse baize,

from which I came out robust and well tempered,

my rustic hide much toughened.


To nature I have owed

more than the lord, the rich man, and the magnate,

for she made him delicate and restless,

and me, with ease, well sturdy.


He covers himself in silk, which does not warm;

I resist inclemency with wool;

he is frightened and fatigued by eating,


while I am happy if I flatter my conscience,

for I fill my belly at every hour,

trusting that there is God and providence.
